answer:
Under the **Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 (the “Act”) of India**, an arbitral award may be set aside on specific grounds enumerated in **Section 34**. The Act provides for a limited scope of judicial review to ensure fairness, legality, and procedural integrity, while preserving the finality and enforceability of arbitration awards.

### Section 34 — Grounds for Setting Aside an Arbitral Award

An arbitral award may be set aside by a court **only if one or more of the following grounds are satisfied**:

> **(1)** The parties have not agreed to submit their dispute to arbitration;  
> **(2)** The arbitration agreement is void or incapable of being performed;  
> **(3)** The subject matter of the dispute is not capable of settlement by arbitration;  
> **(4)** The award deals with a matter beyond the scope of the arbitration agreement;  
> **(5)** The proceedings in the arbitral tribunal were not conducted in accordance with the rules or procedures agreed upon by the parties, and such non-compliance was material to the fairness of the proceedings;  
> **(6)** The award is contrary to public policy;  
> **(7)** The arbitrator has acted in bad faith or has been biased or partial;  
> **(8)** The award has not been made by a competent tribunal, or the tribunal lacks jurisdiction;  
> **(9)** The award was obtained through fraud or corruption;  
> **(10)** The award is contrary to law or public policy (as per Section 34(6));  
> **(11)** The parties have agreed that the award shall be set aside, and such agreement has been made in writing.

---

### Important Notes:

- **Section 34(2)**: A court may not set aside an award unless it is satisfied that one or more of the grounds mentioned above apply.

- **Section 34(6)**: The court may also set aside an award if it finds that the award violates public policy, even if it does not fall under any specific enumerated ground.

- **Section 34(7)**: If a party has already filed an application for setting aside an award and the court has dismissed such an application, then no further action can be taken unless there is new evidence or material change in circumstances.

---

### Additional Context:

The Act follows the principle of **“finality of arbitration”** — it is designed to protect the integrity of arbitral proceedings. The courts are generally reluctant to interfere with awards unless there is a clear and substantial violation of law, fairness, or public policy.

In practice, courts have interpreted “public policy” broadly but strictly, and “bad faith” or “bias” must be clearly established.

---

### Example:

If an arbitrator was found to have accepted bribes from one party, the court may set aside the award under **Section 34(9)** — fraud or corruption.

If a party claims that the arbitration agreement was never validly entered into (e.g., due to lack of consent), then **Section 34(1)** applies.

---

### Conclusion:

The grounds for setting aside an arbitral award are limited and strictly defined under Section 34. Courts must be satisfied that one or more of these grounds exist before they can intervene — ensuring the sanctity of arbitration while preserving justice.

> ✅ **Key takeaway**: The court’s power to set aside an award is very restricted, and judicial review is narrowly construed to protect the finality of arbitral awards under Indian law.
